From ef7a9be75622cdc84ac96e147208677c0f67bdc3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: broken-branch <broken-branch@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 10:10:35 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] Rescan sensors, reconnect the display, log state changes
 once

Rescan sensors when either one is unbound or its chip is gone (driver
unloaded or renumbered; the hwmon directory's "name" is checked, since
hwmon numbers are reused), at most every 10 s. Make the rescan real:
the sensors crate initialises libsensors once per process, so
recreating Sensors never saw a driver that loaded or reloaded later.
Tear libsensors down with sensors_cleanup() and initialise it again,
with every handle dropped first. A configured sensor that never appears
therefore re-initialises libsensors every 10 s; that is cheap. A chip
that is present but momentarily unreadable, such as a GPU in runtime
suspend, is left alone and read again next tick.

Neither sensor being found is no longer fatal at start: the panel shows
dashes and the daemon keeps looking. A display that is absent at start
still fails immediately, which gives the clearest first-run diagnostic;
one that stops accepting frames later is dropped and reopened once per
tick for up to 60 s before giving up, logging each distinct failure
once, so a display that re-enumerates after a hub reset or resume comes
back on its own. This replaces the suspend heuristic, which compared
Instant readings and could not fire: Instant is CLOCK_MONOTONIC, which
stops during suspend.

A sensor's state (reading, not readable, not found, and which chip) is
logged once when it changes instead of every second; this moves the
start-up "using ..." line from bind time to the first read.
